Native Matrix Producers and Function Application
===============================================================================

1. Scope
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The v0.4 native sequence AOT path now admits coefficient-producing matrix
comprehensions, matrix transpose, matrix-vector multiplication, vector dot
products, and invariant scalar vector comprehensions. Algorithms and coefficient
formulas remain Mech source. The compiler follows canonical syntax and typed
operations; it does not select behavior from program or function names.

Function application and expression-bodied definitions are a separate source
normalization step. This work reuses the existing shaped array forms:

  rows := 0..3
  columns := 0..2
  A := [[number/to-f64((i + 1) * (j + 2)) | j <- columns]' | i <- rows]'
  step(x<[f64]>) => <[f64]> := A' ** (A ** x)
  x := [1.5 | j <- columns]'
  y := x |> step
  energy := y · y

<[T]> denotes a vector of element kind T and any length. Runtime shape guards
still enforce the dimensions required by each matrix multiplication.

2. Shapes and ordinary array forms
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Comprehensions concatenate horizontally. A scalar comprehension is a row. Its
transpose is a column. Nesting those columns over an outer generator constructs
a matrix with the inner index on rows and the outer index on columns. The final
transpose in the example gives the usual outer-index rows and inner-index columns.

Each generator currently ranges over an exclusive, ascending u64 range 0..length.
A previously declared range or range alias is also admitted. Matrix dimensions
are snapshotted at construction, and immutable aliases and transpose views retain
the shape. Named function definitions have document scope; parameter and local
bindings shadow globals within the function.

The native typed producer retains zero-length axes and its inferred f64 element
kind. A generator declaring zero rows and n columns produces shape 0 by n, even
when no element is evaluated. The converse n by 0 is also preserved. Existing
resident untyped empty constructors collapse empty axes to 0 by 0; those legacy
constructors are unchanged. This typed native shape contract must be carried into
future authoritative shaped-array HIR work rather than inferred from allocation.

3. Lowering and allocation elimination
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

After application syntax normalization, the canonical AST identifies the two
nested generators, pure entry expression, shape bindings, and matrix consumers.
The compiler retains a shaped producer descriptor and generates a typed Mech
coefficient function with u64 index parameters and one f64 output. Parameter order
follows outer generator then inner generator. This preserves construction error
order and lets the existing numeric graph optimizations apply directly.

Transpose swaps the descriptor's dimensions and index interpretation. Matrix-vector
multiplication checks the vector length and lowers to an existing native traversal
that compiles the coefficient graph into its loop. Matrix coefficients and their
transpose are not materialized. A dot product lowers to the existing dot reduction.
An invariant, pure scalar comprehension lowers to a typed sequence fill.

The native graph receives generic internal matrix traversal, dimension-validation,
and coefficient-domain-validation operations. They are not user-facing benchmark
functions. The existing scheduling, common-subexpression elimination, loop
invariant motion, unsigned interval proofs, induction analysis, SIMD generation,
row blocking, and buffer reuse apply to these graphs. No formula-specific rule is
added by this source lowering.

For integer-indexed coefficients, the backend evaluates each lane's unsigned
prefix in original graph order, retaining interval proofs, induction values,
invariants, common subexpressions, and checked fallback error order. Converted
floating values are then packed, and the remaining floating-point DAG executes
with SIMD. This avoids performing reciprocal, square-root, or other f64 suffix
operations separately in each scalar lane. It changes neither expression topology
nor per-lane floating-point rounding; scalar and tail paths remain scalar.


4. Preserving errors and arithmetic
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Eliminating eager coefficient storage must not defer checked-arithmetic errors
past observable IO or make those errors disappear when an iteration has zero
steps. A domain-validation node therefore remains at the original matrix
construction point. It participates in the source-ordered error/effect chain.

The compiled validator first proves unsigned arithmetic safe over the declared
index bounds. If the conservative proof cannot establish safety, a checked native
scalar traversal validates each entry in source generator order. Empty domains
evaluate no entries. Floating-point operations retain the existing IEEE behavior.

Shape guards reject incompatible vector lengths before the associated product.
The coefficient body retains its arithmetic operations. In particular, multiplying
by an entry computed as 1.0 / denominator is not replaced with vector division:
those expressions can round differently. Any future reassociation requires an
explicit numerical optimization contract rather than an implicit syntax rule.

5. Current boundaries
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The native slice currently requires one plain generator per nested comprehension,
zero-based exclusive u64 ranges, f64 scalar coefficients, and pure scalar arithmetic
with supported conversion and square-root calls. Invariant vector comprehensions
must not depend on their generator index. Matrix consumers are matrix-vector
multiplication, transpose/aliases, and vector dot products.

Filtered, inclusive, stepped, or nonzero-start ranges; matrix materialization;
matrix-matrix products; nonuniform vector comprehensions; and scalar coefficient
captures are not yet lowered. Immutable shape captures in the iteration function
are supported separately. Unsupported forms and unused materialized producers
receive diagnostics. Source normalization is not yet canonical parser/formatter
integration, and diagnostics can identify generated rather than original ranges.

6. Validation
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The matrix normalizer's isolated tests cover rectangular orientation, transpose
and range aliases, document-scope functions, lexical parameter shadowing, duplicate
virtual bindings, inline producers, constructor validation placement, impurity,
quoted markers, scalar parentheses, and unsupported forms.

examples/function-application-prototype/verify_matrix.py independently checks
scalar and SIMD executions, rectangular and empty shapes, SIMD tails, partition
counts, captured dimensions and nested calls, coefficient overflow/division errors
before IO, conservative proof fallback, shape errors, and spectral norm outputs.
Other benchmark source migrations are validated against preserved baseline source
and output. Source line counts apply to the visible Mech program; generated
compiler support does not enter those counts.
